       session‐keyring - session shared process keyring

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  session  keyring  is  a keyring used to anchor keys on behalf of a
       process.  It is typically created by ppaamm__kkeeyyiinniitt(8) when a user logs in
       and a link will be added that refers to the  uusseerr--kkeeyyrriinngg(7).   Option‐
       ally,  PPAAMM(7)  may  revoke  the session keyring on logout.  (In typical
       configurations, PAM does do this revocation.)  The session keyring  has
       the name (description) ___s_e_s.

       A  special  serial  number  value, KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__SSEESSSSIIOONN__KKEEYYRRIINNGG, is defined
       that can be used in lieu of the actual serial  number  of  the  calling
       process’s session keyring.

       From  the  kkeeyyccttll(1) utility, ’@@ss’ can be used instead of a numeric key
       ID in much the same way.

       A process’s session keyring is inherited across cclloonnee(2), ffoorrkk(2),  and
       vvffoorrkk(2).  The session keyring is preserved across eexxeeccvvee(2), even when
       the executable is set‐user‐ID or set‐group‐ID or has capabilities.  The
       session  keyring  is  destroyed when the last process that refers to it
       exits.

       If a process doesn’t have a session keyring when it is accessed,  then,
       under  certain  circumstances,  the uusseerr--sseessssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7) will be at‐
       tached as the session keyring and under others a  new  session  keyring
       will be created.  (See uusseerr--sseessssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7) for further details.)

